f=c?'Look at the attached image and pull out everything worth keeping. Work out\nwhat the image is first, then respond accordingly:\n\n- A calendar, schedule, timetable, agenda or list of events (including a\n  screenshot of a calendar app) -> one task per entry, with date and time, and\n  "activity": "event" on every one of them.\n- An email, message, or meeting invitation -> a task for what it asks of me.\n- A book cover, article, or course -> a parent task plus subtasks spread over a\n  sensible period, sized to how long it realistically takes.\n- A poster, ticket, or invitation -> a dated task for attending.\n- A receipt, form, or letter -> a task for whatever action it requires.\n- A handwritten note or whiteboard -> one task per item written down.\n- Reference material with nothing to do about it (a page of text, a diagram, a\n  business card, a recipe) -> no task, just a note.':'Look at the attached image and write it up as a note I can read later\ninstead of the picture - and write it TWICE, once short and once in full. I\npick one of the two before anything is saved, so both have to stand on their\nown.\n\n- Return exactly ONE entry in "notes" unless the picture genuinely holds\n  several unrelated subjects. Each entry carries both versions: "body" is the\n  short one, "detailed_body" is the full one.\n- "body" - the short version: what the picture IS in one line, then only the\n  handful of facts I would actually want reminding of. Aim for 3-6 short lines\n  and never more than about 80 words.\n- "detailed_body" - the full version: nearly everything the image shows.\n  Transcribe every word of text on it, and keep every figure, date, name,\n  price, reference number, step and heading, laid out the way the picture lays\n  them out. This one is meant to replace the picture, so err on the side of\n  keeping a detail rather than trimming it. Length is not a problem here.\n- The two must not be the same text. If the picture holds so little that the\n  full version would just repeat the short one, still fill both, with\n  "detailed_body" carrying the exact wording as printed.\n- Never state something the picture does not show, in either version.\n- Return "tasks": [] unless the picture shows something with a real date or\n  deadline that I would clearly have to act on - an appointment, a ticket for\n  a dated event, a bill due. Reference material is just a note.'
